# Normalize line endings across OSes — repo stays LF, checkouts adapt.
# Prevents noisy CRLF/LF diffs when authoring on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text types (authored source)
*.html  text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.cff   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch line endings
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.edf   binary
*.dat   binary
*.pdf   binary

# ── Generated name-ledgers: UNION MERGE (D2 · EFFICIENCY-AUDIT-FINDINGS-2026-07-12) ──
# These two are the browser lanes' only way to "list a directory", so they are committed — and every
# doc/brief/changeset-touching PR rewrites them. As JSON they were a conflict ENGINE: 62 of 139 commits
# (45%) touched them, TEN merge commits in 48 h existed purely to re-resolve them, and a conflicted PR
# gets NO CI AT ALL (GitHub builds pull_request runs against the merge commit; if it can't merge, it
# dispatches nothing — silently). Line-oriented + merge=union makes two disjoint additions merge
# automatically. Readers sort+dedupe (tests/list-format.js), and the Node lane's `list == fs` staleness
# assertion still reds on a bad union, so the worst case is a RED you fix with `npm run gen:lists` —
# never a silent no-CI PR. Regenerate, never hand-edit.
tests/docs-ledger-list.txt  merge=union
tests/changes-list.txt      merge=union

# ── The residue ledger: the same conflict engine, one level up (2026-09-13) ──
# briefs/RESIDUE.md is append-only at ONE end by rule (rows are added and closed, never edited or
# deleted — CLAUDE.md §📌), so any two PRs that each file a row conflict on the same tail bytes by
# construction. Measured 2026-09-12: 19 of 32 commits to main (59 %, one full-drain day — not a
# steady-state rate) touched it; one PR was rebased three times for this and nothing else, and every
# resolution was "keep both sides' rows" with no judgement. That is exactly what union does. The
# worst case is a doubled row, and docs-ledger check8c (row ids unique) reds on it — never a silent
# merge. THE BOUNDARY: union can express an append, never an EDIT — and the ledger has one legal edit,
# closing a row by rewriting its state cell. Measured 2026-09-13 in a synthetic repo: closing a row in
# the MIDDLE while another PR appends merges clean and correct; closing a row NEAR THE TAIL (the usual
# one — recent rows get closed) while another PR appends keeps BOTH lines, `| k | OPEN |` and
# `| k | fixed #1 |`, with no conflict raised. That is a CI red (check8c) after the merge instead of a
# local conflict before it: the driver is not broken, delete the stale copy. Local merges/rebases only
# (rebase-safe treats the file as source and now finds no conflict); GitHub's server-side merge and
# Kodiak's update-branch do not honour this attribute, so a BEHIND PR may still need one local merge.
briefs/RESIDUE.md           merge=union
